use std
into meshpack.amni

Namespace meshpack; imports math. Prepares and compresses meshes for the glTF extensions EXT_meshopt_compression and KHR_meshopt_compression: a vertex cache order for triangles (meshoptimizer's greedy optimizer with its tuned score table, after Tom Forsyth's "Linear-speed vertex cache optimisation", 2006), a vertex fetch order, and encoders for triangle streams (mode 1, version 1), index streams (mode 2, version 1) and vertex attribute streams (mode 0, version 0) that `meshopt::decode` and the meshoptimizer reference decoder read back. Index lists are whole-number slices with three entries per triangle; every output list is a growable list passed by reference that the function appends to.

Constants: `CACHE = 16` (the cache size the order is tuned for); `CACHE_SCORE`, 17 reals: 0, 0.779, 0.791, 0.789, 0.981, 0.843, 0.726, 0.847, 0.882, 0.867, 0.799, 0.642, 0.613, 0.600, 0.568, 0.372, 0.234; `LIVE_SCORE`, 9 reals: 0, 0.995, 0.713, 0.450, 0.404, 0.059, 0.005, 0.147, 0.006.

`acmr(idx, cache) real` simulates a first-in first-out vertex cache of `cache` entries over the index list and gives the misses per triangle (0 for no triangles). The vertex count is the largest index plus 1. A miss counter starts at 0 and every vertex's stamp at minus a big number; an index whose vertex has a stamp of at least the miss counter minus `cache` is a hit; otherwise it is a miss: its stamp becomes the miss counter, then the counter goes up by 1.

`vscore(pos, live) real` is a vertex's score: CACHE_SCORE at pos plus 1 (pos is -1 when not cached) plus LIVE_SCORE at the smaller of live and 8.

`cache_order(idx, nv, out)` appends the triangles of idx to the whole-number list out in an order that reuses a small vertex cache, each triangle keeping its own three indices in their order; nv is the vertex count. With t triangles: live[v] counts the triangles that use vertex v; the triangles of each vertex sit in one shared list, vertex v's range starting at the sum of live over the vertices before it, triangles in index order, and live[v] is the length of the part of the range still in use. Nothing is done for no triangles. Every vertex's score starts at `vscore(-1, live)` and every triangle's score is the sum of its three corners' scores; no triangle is emitted yet. Two fixed whole-number arrays of CACHE + 4 entries hold the cache and the next cache (no list is made inside the loop); the cache holds 0 entries at first. The current triangle starts at 0 and a cursor at 1. While there is a current triangle: emit it (its three indices go to out, it is marked emitted and its score becomes 0). Write the next cache: its three corners in order, then every entry of the cache that is none of them, in order, counting the written entries as w; swap the two arrays and the cache now holds the smaller of w and CACHE entries. For each of the triangle's corners v, the triangle is taken out of v's range by moving the range's last live entry into its place, and live[v] goes down by 1. Then best is none with a best score of 0, and for each of the w written entries in order, v: skip it when live[v] is 0; its position is its place when below CACHE and -1 otherwise; its new score is `vscore(position, live[v])`, and with diff the new score minus its old score (which it then keeps), every triangle in v's live range gets diff added to its score, and when that score is above the best score the triangle becomes best and its score the best score. The next current triangle is best; when there is none, the cursor moves on past emitted triangles and the next current triangle is the cursor's, or there is none when the cursor reaches t.

`fetch_remap(idx, nv, remap) int` fills the whole-number list remap (made nv long, every entry -1 first) with each vertex's new number in the order the index list first uses it, and gives how many vertices are used.

`optimize(idx, nv, out, remap) int` runs `cache_order` on idx into a fresh list, then `fetch_remap` on that with remap, appends the reordered list's indices to out as their new numbers, and gives the used vertex count.

`remap_bytes(data, stride, remap, used, out)` appends used times stride bytes to the byte list out: vertex v's stride bytes from data go to the slot remap[v] whenever remap[v] is 0 or more.

`zig32(d) int` wraps d to a signed 32-bit number (add 2 to the power 31, keep the low 32 bits, subtract 2 to the power 31) and zigzags it: twice it when 0 or more, otherwise minus twice it minus 1. `put_varint(out, v)` appends v as unsigned LEB128: 7 bits a byte, lowest first, every byte but the last with 128 added.

`tri_stream(idx, table, n_table, codes, data, freq)` runs the triangle encoder over idx, appending one code byte per triangle to the byte list codes and the extra bytes to the byte list data; only the first n_table entries of the byte slice table are searched, and freq (a whole-number list of 256 entries) counts every aux byte of a triangle that took the "new triangle" path with fea 0 and no reset. State: next and last start at 0; an edge fifo of 16 pairs and a vertex fifo of 16 whole numbers all start at -1, each with a write offset starting at 0; pushing writes at the offset then moves it on by 1 modulo 16; entry i of a fifo means the slot (offset - 1 - i) modulo 16, the newest being entry 0. `edge_find(a, b, c)` gives i times 4 plus r for the first entry i from 0 to 15 whose pair is (a, b) with r 0, else (b, c) with r 1, else (c, a) with r 2, and -1 when no entry matches. `vert_find(v)` gives the first entry i holding v or -1. For each triangle (a, b, c), with fer = `edge_find(a, b, c)`:

- When fer is 0 or more and fer shifted right by 2 is below 15: fe is fer shifted right by 2; rotate the corners by fer bitwise-and 3 (rotation 1 makes (b, c, a), rotation 2 makes (c, a, b)) and call them (a, b, c) again. fc = `vert_find(c)`; fec is fc when fc is from 1 to 12; otherwise 0 when c equals next (and next goes up by 1); otherwise 15. When fec is 15 and c plus 1 equals last, fec becomes 13 and last becomes c; else when fec is 15 and c equals last plus 1, fec becomes 14 and last becomes c. The code byte is fe times 16 plus fec. When fec is 15, put `zig32(c - last)` as a varint into data and last becomes c. When fec is 0 or at least 13, push c on the vertex fifo. Push the edges (c, b) and then (a, c).
- Otherwise rotate so that a equals next when b or c does: rotation 1 when b equals next, else 2 when c equals next, else none. reset is true when (a, b, c) is (0, 1, 2) and next is above 0; then next becomes 0 and every vertex fifo slot becomes -1. fb = `vert_find(b)` and fc = `vert_find(c)`, both looked up now. fea is 0 when a equals next (next goes up by 1), else 15. feb is fb plus 1 when fb is from 0 to 13; else 0 when b equals next (next goes up by 1); else 15. fec likewise from fc and c. The aux byte is feb times 16 plus fec. When fea is 0 and not reset, freq of the aux byte goes up by 1, and when the aux byte is among the first n_table entries of the table at entry k with k below 14, the code byte is 240 plus k. Otherwise the code byte is 254 plus (1 when fea is 15) and the aux byte goes into data. Then, in this order, when fea is 15, feb is 15 and fec is 15, put `zig32` of that corner minus last as a varint into data and last becomes that corner. Push a on the vertex fifo; push b when feb is 0 or 15; push c when fec is 0 or 15. Push the edges (b, a), (c, b) and (a, c).

`encode_triangles(idx, out)` appends a mode 1 stream for idx (whose length is a multiple of 3) to the byte list out. First `tri_stream` runs with an empty table into scratch lists, only to count freq. The table has 16 entries: entry 0 is 0; entries 1 to 13 are the 13 most counted aux bytes other than 0 whose two nibbles are both below 15 and whose count is above 0, most counted first and the smaller byte first on ties; every entry left is 0. Then `tri_stream` runs again with that table and n_table 14 into fresh codes and data. The stream is the byte 0xe1, the codes, the data and the 16 table bytes.

`encode_sequence(idx, out)` appends a mode 2 stream to out: the byte 0xd1; then with two baselines both 0 and current 0, for every index: cd is the index minus baseline current, and when the absolute value of cd is at least 30 current flips between 0 and 1; v is `zig32` of the index minus baseline current; put v times 2 plus current as a varint, and baseline current becomes the index. Then 4 zero bytes.

`zig8(d) int` of a byte difference: with d kept to 8 bits, (d times 2) kept to 8 bits, bitwise-xor 255 when d is at least 128.

`group_size(g, w) int` measures how many bytes a group of 16 deltas (a slice) takes at bit width w: width 0 is 0 when every delta is 0 and 1000 otherwise; width 8 is 16; widths 2 and 4 are 16 times w over 8 plus the number of deltas at least 2 to the power w minus 1.

`encode_attributes(data, count, stride, out) bool` appends a mode 0 version 0 stream for count elements of stride bytes from data to out, and gives false (appending nothing) when stride is not a multiple of 4, is below 4 or above 256, when count is below 0 or when data holds fewer than count times stride bytes. The byte 0xa0 comes first. The previous element starts as a copy of element 0 (stride zeros when count is 0). The largest block is the smaller of 256 and (8192 integer-divided by stride, rounded down to a multiple of 16). Elements go in blocks of the smaller of the largest block and the elements left. For a block of n elements, with n16 being n rounded up to a multiple of 16: for each byte position k from 0 to stride minus 1, the deltas are n16 bytes, delta i being `zig8` of element i's byte k minus the byte k before it (the previous element's for i 0) for i below n and 0 after. Then come (n16 over 16 plus 3) over 4 header bytes, all 0 first, and the groups: for each group g of 16 deltas the best width index is 3 to start, then each index 0, 1 and 2 in order replaces it when its `group_size` with widths 0, 2, 4, 8 is strictly smaller than the best so far, or equal to it while its width is the width chosen for the previous group of this byte position in this block (none for the first group) and the best so far is not width 8; header byte g integer-divided by 4 gets the index shifted left by 2 times (g mod 4) set in it. The group's bytes: width 0 none; width 8 the 16 deltas; widths 2 and 4 pack 8 over w deltas per byte in order, each one as the smaller of itself and the sentinel 2 to the power w minus 1, with the earlier delta in the higher bits, then every delta at least the sentinel as a whole byte, in order. The header bytes stand before the block's groups for that byte position. After the block the previous element is the block's last element. At the end come the larger of stride and 32, minus stride, zero bytes, then element 0's stride bytes (zeros when count is 0). True.

`encode(data, count, stride, mode, out) bool` mirrors `meshopt::decode`: mode 0 calls `encode_attributes`; modes 1 and 2 read count indices of stride bytes each (2 or 4, little-endian, failing otherwise or when data is short) and call `encode_triangles` (failing when count is not a multiple of 3) or `encode_sequence`; any other mode fails. On failure nothing is appended.
